== The Ancient Law and Unique Bond of Marriage

=== §. 79. #emph[Of the ancient Law of marriage.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 5. 31.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For this cause shall a man leave his father and mother, and shall be joined unto his wife: and they two shall be one flesh.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

The same points which were before laid down, concerning the near union of man and wife, and of Christ and the Church, are here further confirmed by the ancient law of marriage: which the Apostle doth the rather mention, because it followeth upon that text, whereunto he alluded in the former verse. For when #emph[Moses] had alleged these words of #emph[Adam] concerning #emph[Eve, This is bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh,] he #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 23, 24.] addeth this law, #emph[Therefore shall a man leave his father,] etc. In this place these words have both a #emph[literal] and a #emph[mystical] sense. A #emph[literal] of man and wife. A #emph[mystical] of Christ and the Church. The main thing which the Apostle aimeth at, is to #footnote[Inference.] show how nearly man and wife are linked together: that thereby they may the rather be moved to perform those mutual and several duties which they owe each to other. But because he propounded to husbands and wives the examples of Christ and the Church, as patterns and motives to them, to do their duty, he applieth that which was first spoken of man and wife, unto Christ and his Church, to show that there being so fit a resemblance betwixt these two couples, the pattern propounded is the more pertinent to the purpose, and the reason enforced from thence the more forcible.

Because the opening of the literal sense will give great light to the mystery, I will first handle this text according to the meaning of the letter.

The first clause #emph[(for this cause)] implieth a necessary connection #footnote[ἀντὶ τούτου.] with that which went before. The near union of man and wife, as well as of Christ and his Church, was before noted. A wife was said to be as the #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 28. #emph[Gen.] 2. 23.] #emph[body] of a man, yea as #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 28. #emph[Gen.] 2. 23.] #emph[himself. Adam] called her his #emph[flesh and bones.] Hereupon both #emph[Moses] and #emph[Paul] infer, #emph[Therefore,] or, #emph[For this cause shall a man leave father.] Because man and wife are so near by Gods institution, they must also be most dear each to other in their mutual affection.

The #emph[man] (meaning an husband) is here in particular mentioned, #footnote[Meaning of the words.] because at the first making of this Law the woman was brought to him to see how he would like her: and having cast his affection on her, he was to be bound hereby to continue that good liking towards her: as also because of the preheminency which man hath above his wife. Yet is not the man only tied hereby, but the wife also: the nature and rule of relation requireth as much: if a man must inseparably cleave to his wife, the wife must answerably cleave to her husband.

These words #emph[(shall leave father and mother)] are neither #emph[generally] to be taken of all duties, as if no duty were to be performed to parents by children after they are married: nor #emph[simply,] as if indeed parents were utterly to be forsaken: but they are meant,

1. Of that #emph[daily service] which children under their parents government perform unto them, seeking to please them in all things. When children are married, then their daily attendance must be upon their wives, or husbands, taking care how #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 33, 34.] to please them.

2. Of #emph[erecting a new family:] for which end their parents house must be left, and the husband and wife must dwell each with other.

3. Of the #emph[difference to be put betwixt parents, and wife or husband.] So as if by any inevitable occasion it should so fall out, that a man must leave his parent, or his wife (as in case parent and wife were both giving up the Ghost, and in places so far remote, as the husband could not possibly be with both, yet both instantly desired his company) by this Law he must leave his parent, and cleave to his wife.

Hereby then the bond of marriage is declared to be the most inviolable bond that can be. For all men know, that the bond betwixt parent and child is a firm and inviolable bond: but the bond betwixt husband and wife is more firm and inviolable.

To set forth the firmness of the marriage bond he addeth this emphatical phrase, #emph[shall be joined,] (or as the word properly, according to the natural notation thereof signifieth, #emph[shall be glued)] #footnote[προσκολληθήσεται. κόλλα. Glue.] to his wife. Things well glued together are as fast, firm, and close as if they were one entire piece. Yea we observe by experience that a table will oft times cleave in the whole wood, before it will part asunder where it is glued: so as an husband ought to be as firm to his wife as to himself: and she to him.

Fitly doth this agree with that which followeth #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] Our English cannot well express the Greek #footnote[ἔσονται οἱ δύο εἰς σάρκα μίαν.] in good sense word for word (which is thus, #emph[they two shall be into,] or #emph[in one flesh)] the meaning is, They which were two before marriage, by the bond of marriage are brought into one flesh, to be even as one flesh: as nearly united, as the parts of the same body, and the same flesh. This unity is not in regard of carnal copulation (for if they be married they are #emph[one flesh,] #footnote[#emph[Matrimonium non facit coitus, sed voluntas. Chrysost. in Mat. hom.] 32.] though they never know one another) nor in regard of procreation, because one child cometh from them both (for though they never have child, yet are they one flesh) but in regard of Gods institution, who hath set it down for a law, and as another nature, that man and wife should be so near one to another. Their consent in marriage (by virtue of Gods institution) maketh them to be #emph[one flesh.]

Well doth our English note the emphasis of the original in this particle THEY #emph[(they two)] which showeth that the bond #footnote[οἱ δύο.] of marriage knitteth only #emph[two] together: one man, and one woman, and no more.

This Law setteth forth the #emph[Union betwixt man and wife.] #footnote[Sum.]

Therein three things are noted concerning the state of marriage.

1. The praeeminency of it #emph[(a man shall leave father and mother.)]

2. The firmness of it #emph[(and be joined to his wife.)]

3. The nearness of it #emph[(they two shall be one flesh.)]

=== §. 80. #emph[Of preferring husband or wife before parents.]

The first point showeth, that

#emph[A wife, or an husband must be preferred before parents.] The #footnote[Doct.] examples of #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 14.] #emph[Leah] and #emph[Rachel,] yea and of #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19. 11.] #emph[Michal,] are commendable in this respect.

1. The bond of marriage is more ancient, more firm, more #footnote[Reason.] near. There was husband and wife before there was parent and child: and there is a time when parents & children may depart one from another, and that while both live: but no time, wherein man and wife may part asunder till death part them. And children though they come from the flesh of their parents, yet are made two (so as #emph[of one are two)] but husbands and wives though they were two before, yet are made one (so as #emph[of two is one.)]

What wrong then do such parents unto their children, as #footnote[Use.] keep them, even after they are married, so strait under subjection, #footnote[Parents may not keep their children being married too straight.] as they cannot freely perform such duty as they ought to their husband, or their wife? This is more then a parents authority reacheth unto. Yet many think that their children owe as much service to them after they are married as before: which is directly against this law.

Greater is the wrong, and more sinful is the practise of #footnote[Nor keep them from husband or wife.] such as keep their children from their husbands, or from their wives. The match (say they) falleth out much worse then we looked for. But this should have been looked to more carefully before hand. After marriage it is too late to seek such a redress.

On the other side, there be many children who so respect #footnote[Children may not prefer their parents before husband or wife. #emph[Apud Romanos sponsa obtecta flammeo capite quum extulisset pedem è paternis aedibus, vertebatur in gyrum, itaque ducebatur in domum sponsi, nesciret redeundi viam ad aedes parentum. Erasm.]] their parents, as they neglect their husband or their wife. Some husbands will bestow what they can on their parents, and keep their wives very bare, suffering them to want necessaries; not caring how they vex and grieve them so they please their parents. Some wives also will privily purloin from their husbands to bestow on their parents.

Others can never tarry out of their parents houses, but as oft as they can, go thither. The ancient Romans, to show how unmeet this was, had a custom to cover the brides face with a yellow veil, and so soon as she was out of her fathers house to turn her about and about, and so to carry her to the house of her husband, that she might not know the way to her fathers house again. All those pretenses of love to parents are more preposterous then pious: and natural affection beareth more sway in such, then true religion. Their pretence of piety to parents is no just excuse for that injury they do to husband and wife.

=== §. 81. #emph[Of the firmness of the matrimonial bond.]

The second point concerning the firmness of the marriage knot in these words #emph[(shall be joined to his wife)] affordeth two doctrines.

1. #emph[Man and wife must associate themselves together by continual] #footnote[See #emph[Treat.] 2. #emph[part.] 2. §. 14.] #emph[cohabitation:] for this end they leave their parents family, and erect a new family.

2. #emph[Man and wife are joined together by an inviolable bond.] It #footnote[See #emph[Treat.] 2. #emph[part.] 2. §. 2.] must never be cut asunder till death cut it. Body and soul must be severed one from another before husband and wife.

Be careful therefore to preserve this indissoluble knot: and so live together, as with comfort you may live together, because you may not part.

=== §. 82. #emph[Of two only to be joined together in marriage.]

The third point concerning the nearness of man and wife, in these words #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] affordeth two other doctrines.

1. #emph[Marriage can be but betwixt two,] one man, and one woman: for it is impossible that more then two should so nearly, and firmly be joined together, as man and wife are. Every word almost in this law proveth this doctrine. For it saith #emph[a man,] not men: to #emph[a wife,] not to wives: to #emph[his] wife, not to anothers wife: #emph[two,] not more then two: #emph[they] two, not any two: #emph[one flesh,] not many fleshes.

#strong[#emph[Object.] This particle #emph[(two)] is not in the law as #emph[Moses] recordeth #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 24.] it.]

#emph[Answ.] It is there necessarily implied, for at that time there were but two in the world: God then speaking of them, meaneth but two. The same spirit that guided #emph[Moses,] guided also the #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 5.] Evangelists, and the #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] Apostles: so as by their inserting of this particle #emph[(two)] it is certain that it was intended by #emph[Moses:] as the particle #emph[(only)] which Christ putteth into this text, #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 6. 13. #emph[Mat.] 4. 10.] #emph[him only shalt thou serve.]

#strong[#emph[Quest.] Why did God at first make but one man, and one woman?]

#emph[Answ.] The #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 15.] Prophet answereth, #emph[that he might seek a godly seed.]

If therefore there be more then two, it is an adulterous seed which proceedeth from thence.

=== §. 83. #emph[Of Polygamy and Bigamy.]

Can Polygamy (the having of many wives) or Bigamy (the having of two wives at once) have any good warrant against such an express law? Are not both of them against the first institution of marriage, so as we may say, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 8.] #emph[from the beginning it was not so?] Yea also and against #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 17. 17. #emph[Lev.] 18. 18.] other particular laws? #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 4. 19. #emph[Magis pertinere ad nuptiarum bonum, non vnum & multas, sed vnum & vnam, satis indicat ipsa prima diuinitus facta coniugum copula, etc. Aug. de Nupt. & concup. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9. #emph[Gen.] 2. 18. #emph[Gen.] 16. 5. & 21. 11.] #emph[Lamech] one of #emph[Cains] cursed stock was the first that we read of to have presumed against that ancient law.

#strong[#emph[Object.] Afterwards many Patriarchs, and other Saints took that liberty unto themselves.]

#emph[Answ.] It was their sin, and a great blemish in them. The common error of the time, & their unsatiable desire of increase made them fall into it. Many inconveniences followed thereupon: neither can it be thought but that much mischief must needs follow upon having more wives then one: for whereas God at first made a wife to be as an help unto man, two, or more wives cannot but be a great grief and vexation unto him by reason of that emulation that is betwixt them. Through #emph[Hagars] means was #emph[Sarah] stirred against #emph[Abraham,] and #emph[Abraham] grieved at #emph[Sarahs] words. Though #emph[Leah] and #emph[Rachel] were sisters, yet great were their emulations: the like whereof is noted of #emph[Peninnah] and many others.

Considering the heinousness of this sin, our laws #footnote[#emph[Stat.] 1. #emph[Jacob.] 11.] have justly made it felony for a man to have more wives then one, or a woman more husbands.

=== §. 84. #emph[Of the near conjunction of man and wife together.]

2. #emph[The nearest of all other are husband and wife one to another.] #footnote[Doctr.] Every clause in the forenamed law proveth as much.

1. #emph[Parents] must be #emph[left] for wife: who nearer then parent and child? If man and wife be nearer then the nearest, then they are the nearest of all.

2. A man is #emph[glued] to his wife. This metaphor setteth forth the #emph[nearness] of a thing as well as the #emph[firmness] of it: for things glued together are as one entire thing.

3. Man and wife are #emph[one flesh:] many of one are made two, but no #emph[two] so nearly and truly made #emph[one] as man and wife.

As God hath limited a propinquity, and unity of things, so #footnote[Reason.] are they to be accounted: but God hath thus nearly knit man and wife together, and made them one flesh. #emph[Those whom] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 6.] GOD #emph[hath joined together,] saith Christ of man and wife: in which respect matrimonial conjunction is called #emph[the covenant] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 2. 17.] #emph[of God:] so as this covenant cannot be released by any, no not by the mutual consent of man and wife #emph[(Those whom] GOD #emph[hath joined together, let no man put asunder)] yet may many other covenants made betwixt party and party, be released and disannulled by mutual consent of both parties.

1. This showeth that the transgressions of man and wife #footnote[Uses.] one against another are of all the most heinous, more then of #footnote[1\. Mutual transgressions of man and wife most heinous.] friend, fellow, brother, child, parent or any other. Who would not cry #emph[fie upon] that child that hates his parent, or #emph[fie upon] that parent that hates his child? The heathen & savages would not think them worthy of human society. What then may be thought of the man that hateth his wife, or the wife that hateth her husband? Apply this to all other transgressions: and well note how #emph[the Lord is a witness] thereof. #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 14.]

2. This also showeth how monstrous a thing it is to sow #footnote[2\. Monstrous to make debate betwixt man and wife.] any seeds of discord, and stir debate betwixt man and wife. The devils instruments they are therein, and a diabolical spirit is in them. For Satan most laboureth to unloose those knots which the Lord knitteth most firmly. Children of several venters, and several friends of each party, are much faulty herein. #emph[Cursed be they all before the Lord.]

3. #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 26. 19.] This near conjunction betwixt man and wife is a great #footnote[3\. All duties betwixt man and wife to be cheerfully performed. #emph[Isa.] 58. 7.] motive to stir them both up, cheerfully to perform all the duties which God requireth of either of them. For thereby they do duty, and show kindness to their own flesh. No man may #emph[hide himself from his own flesh] at large: that is, no man may neglect any duty of mercy, or justice to his neighbour who is of the same stock that he is: shall then an husband or wife hide themselves from one another who in the nearest respect that possibly can be are #emph[one flesh?] not because they come #emph[from] one flesh, but because they come #footnote[εἰς σάρκα μίαν.] #emph[into] one flesh.

Hitherto of the literal sense of this verse.

The mystical followeth.

=== §. 85. #emph[Of the matrimonial conjunction of Christ and the Church.]

The forenamed ancient marriage law is here applied mystically #footnote[#emph[Quod per historiam impletum est in Adam, per prophetiam significat Christum qui reliquit patrem, etc. Aug. de Gen. lib.] 2. #emph[c.] 24.] to Christ and the Church, as is evident by the next verse, where the Apostle having reference to this verse saith, #emph[This is a great mystery.] There is then a mystery contained in it. But of what, or of whom is that mystery? The Apostle himself maketh answer, in these words: #emph[I speak concerning Christ and the Church.]

The mystery in general is this,

#emph[Christ and the Church are to one another as husband and wife.]

The particulars of this mystery are these.

The matrimonial conjunction betwixt Christ & the Church is a most

+ Preheminent
+ Firm
+ Near

conjunction.

First of the general.

The many espousal, and matrimonial titles, which in Scripture are given to Christ and the Church in mutual relation of one to another, evidently show that they are joined together by the honourable, inseparable and inviolable bond of marriage: He is styled a #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 3. 29.] #emph[bridegroom,] she a #emph[bride:] he #footnote[#emph[Cant.] 1. 13, 15.] #emph[Well-beloved,] she #emph[love:] he an #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 11. 2.] #emph[husband,] she a #emph[wife:] he an #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 23.] #emph[Head,] she the #emph[Body:] both #footnote[#emph[& vers.] 31.] #emph[one flesh.]

2. All things requisite to join man and wife together, do fitly concur betwixt Christ and the Church.

1. They are persons fit to be joined. Though Christ be #footnote[The things which make Christ and the Church fit to be husband and wife.] God, yet for #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 17. 19.] this end he became man: and though the Church were impure, yet for this end is she cleansed and sanctified.

2. They have their parents consent: for God is the #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 20. 17.] common father of both. And God hath given #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 8. 32.] Christ to the Church, and the #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 6. 39.] Church to Christ.

3. They have given their #footnote[#emph[Cant.] 2. 16.] mutual consent each to other.

4. He #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 23, 24.] beareth an husband like affection to her, and she is willing to yield a wife-like subjection to him.

5. He hath #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 4. 8.] given her many favours and gifts as pledges of his love: and she in testimony of her faithfulness was under the Law circumcised, and is under the Gospel baptized: and doth bind her self with all the sacred bonds and covenants which God to that purpose hath sanctified.

6. He hath prepared #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 14. 3.] places of habitation for them both together, and #footnote[#emph[Rev.] 22. 17, 20.] she earnestly desireth to be with him.

Behold another evidence of Christs admirable love to the #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] Church, and of the near union betwixt Christ and her. The #footnote[An high favour to be the spouse to Christ.] former was that she was his #emph[body.] This, that she is his #emph[wife:] well might the Church say as #emph[Abigail] did, #emph[Behold, let thine handmaid be a servant to wash the feet of the servants of my] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 25. 41.] #emph[Lord:] and as the prodigal child, #emph[Make me as one of thine hired] #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 15. 19.] #emph[servants:] or as the Baptist, #emph[I am not worthy to stoop down to] #footnote[#emph[Mar.] 1. 7.] #emph[unloose thy shoe-latchets.] What a favour then is it to be made his spouse, his wife, his Queen. Great was the favour which #emph[Ahasuerus] showed to #emph[Esther,] when he made her his wife: he was a great Monarch, reigning #emph[from India to Ethiopia over] 127 #emph[provinces:] but #emph[Esther] was a poor orphan and captive: yet was not this favour comparable to Christs: for there was no such disparity and inequality betwixt #emph[Ahasuerus] and #emph[Esther,] as betwixt Christ and the Church: neither is #emph[Esthers] advancement to be compared with the Churches: and yet there was some cause in #emph[Esther] to move #emph[Ahasuerus] to do what he did, for she was very beautiful, and lovely, and worthy to be loved: but #footnote[§. 33, 34.] in the Church when Christ first cast his love on her, there was no such thing. No pattern of love can be given any way comparable to this.

Let the Church therefore, and all that profess themselves #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] to be of the Church, take such notice hereof, as they may endeavour #footnote[#emph[Direction.] How Christs spouse must carry her self.] to carry themselves worthy of this honour and advancement: not to wax proud and insolent thereupon, but to despise all vain and worldly toys: to answer love with love, as the Church is set forth in #emph[Solomons] song; to be subject to her husband, to reverence and obey him, and to perform all duties appertaining to such a wife: seeking by all good means to maintain the honour of her place. The Church is made a pattern of duty to all wives: if she should fail, greater inconvenience would follow from thence, then from #emph[Vashtis] #footnote[#emph[Est.] 1. 16. #emph[etc.]] disobedience.

This is the rather to be regarded because it is not only a #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.]] matter of instruction but of trial also, showing both what they #footnote[#emph[Trial.] Who are Christs spouse.] which are of the Church ought to do, and also #footnote[§. 24.] what indeed they will do. Wherefore no profane person that lightly esteemeth the Lord Jesus, no Idolater that casteth his love on other husbands, no swearer or blasphemer that dishonoreth the great Name of Jesus, none that any way are rebellious against him, none that hate, scorn, scoff, or hurt any of his members, can have any comfort in this advancement of the Church, because they have no part therein, nor right thereunto.

But great is that comfort which the true Saints may receive #footnote[4\. #emph[Use.]] therein. For by virtue of this matrimonial bond, #footnote[#emph[Consolation.] The privileges of Christs spouse.]

1. Christ is made a yokefellow with his Church, and her companion. Under all the burdens which are laid upon her, he putteth his shoulder to make it the more easy: yea, the great burdens of Gods wrath, the curse of the law, and sin the cause thereof, hath he so taken on him, as he hath clean freed his Church from them, because they would else have crushed her down to hell.

2. Christ is as her champion to answer all challenges sent unto her, as her advocate to plead and answer all the complaints that shall be made against her, as her surety to discharge all her debts: the Church being covert-baron under Christ, he is as her self, all in all for her, and to her.

3. All his honours, goods, privileges are hers: she hath #footnote[#emph[Ecclesia omnem sponsi potestatem tenet. Aug. contr. Don. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 1.] a right to them, and her part in them, she is a coheir with him #emph[(Rom.] 8. 17.) a Queen because he a King #emph[(Psal.] 45. 9.) and all glorious, as was noted, vers. 27.

4. He will assuredly perform all the offices of an husband, as to love her, bear with her, provide for her, with the like. Able he is to do all, for he is omnipotent: willing also he must needs be, because willingly he hath taken upon him this place: he hath made himself a pattern to other husbands: will he not then do that himself which he requireth of others?

If ever any wife might receive comfort in a match, the Church may receive comfort in this match.

The benefit of this match will yet more lively appear by a particular consideration of the three forenamed properties of this matrimonial bond, the #emph[preheminency, firmness,] and #emph[nearness] thereof.

=== §. 86. #emph[Of Christs leaving his Father and mother for his spouse.]

I. The preheminency of the matrimonial bond betwixt Christ and the Church herein appeareth, that

#emph[Christ left his father and his mother for his spouse the Church.] As Christ is God, God is his father; as man, the Virgin #emph[Mary] was his mother. Now the leaving of his father must be taken only by way of resemblance, in that he came from the place of his fathers habitation, to the place where his Spouse was. The Scripture saith, that he #emph[was in the bosom of his father:] #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 1. 18.] #emph[by him, as one brought up with him, his daily delight, rejoicing always] #footnote[#emph[Prov.] 8. 30.] #emph[before him:] yet #emph[descended he into the lowest parts of the earth] #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 4. 9.] #emph[where his spouse was. He came out from the father, and came into] #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 16. 28.] #emph[the world.]

But truly and properly did he prefer his Spouse before his mother. For when he was instructing his Spouse, and his mother came to interrupt him, he said to his mother, #emph[who is my] #footnote[#emph[Mark.] 3. 33, 34.] #emph[mother?] and to his Spouse, #emph[behold my mother.]

Of the #footnote[Use.] same mind must the Church, and all that are of the Church be unto Christ: she must #emph[forget her own people, and fathers] #footnote[#emph[Imitation.] All to be forsaken for Christ. #emph[Psal.] 45. 10.] #emph[house.] Seeing Christ hath gone before us, and given us so good an example, what an high point of ingratitude would it be for us, to prefer father, mother, or any other before Christ our husband? Note what he saith in this case, #emph[He that loveth] #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 10. 37.] #emph[father or mother more then me, is not worthy of me.] And again, #emph[If any come unto me, and hate not his father and mother, he cannot] #footnote[#emph[Luke] 14. 26. expounded. How parents to be hated for Christ.] #emph[be mine.] To #emph[hate] here, is to be so far from preferring father & mother before Christ, as rather then not to love Christ, to hate father and mother. Or, so entirely to love Christ above all, as our love of parents in comparison thereof to be an hatred. Thus #emph[Levi said unto his father and mother, I have not seen him:] #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 33. 9.] #emph[for they observed the word, and kept the covenant of Christ.]

This then is our duty, that we suffer not any natural affection and dotage on our parents to swallow up that love we owe to Christ, as #emph[Pharaohs ill-favoured and lean-fleshed kine eat] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 41. 4.] #emph[up the seven well-favoured and fat kine.] How much less should any love of this world, of the profits, promotions, or pleasures of this world, draw away our hearts from Christ; should we not rather say and do as the Apostles did, #emph[Behold, we have forsaken]#footnote[#emph[Matth.] 19. 27.] #emph[all and followed Christ?]

=== §. 87. #emph[Of the indissoluble union betwixt Christ and the Church.]

II. The firmness of that bond whereby Christ and the Church are said to be #emph[glued] together, is greater and more inviolable then that whereby man and wife are joined together: Death parteth man and wife: but death cannot make a diremption betwixt Christ and the Church: so as we may well from this metaphor infer, that #emph[Christ and the Church are inseparably] #footnote[#emph[Hosea] 2. 19.] #emph[knit together. I will betroth thee unto me for ever,] saith Christ unto the Church. The covenant which Christ maketh with his Church, is an #emph[everlasting] covenant. #emph[The mountains shall depart,] #footnote[#emph[Isa.] 61. 8. #emph[&] 54. 10.] #emph[and the hills be removed, before his kindness shall depart from the Church.]

The steadfastness and unchangeableness of his will, is the #footnote[Reason.] only cause thereof. Whom he loveth, #emph[he loveth unto the end.] #footnote[#emph[Joh.] 13. 1.] #emph[His gifts and calling are without repentance.] He is not like the #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 11. 29.] hard hearted Jews, who upon every slight occasion would put away their wives. #emph[The Lord hateth putting away.] Though #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 16.] therefore the Church, through her weakness, do depart from him, and play the harlot, #emph[yet return again to me,] saith the #footnote[#emph[Jer.] 3. 1.] Lord.

Learn we by this pattern to cleave close unto the Lord, #footnote[#emph[Use.] Cleave to Christ.] which is a duty most due to Christ who cleaveth so close to us, and therefore oft #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 10. 20. #emph[&] 13. 4. #emph[Acts] 11. 23.] expressed in the Scripture.

Three virtues there are which are of special use to this purpose, Faith, Hope, Love.

#emph[Faith] is the hand whereby we lay fast hold on Christ, and as it were knit him to our selves, as he by his Spirit knitteth us to himself. This maketh us rest and repose our selves on him for all needful things: and not to leave him for any thing.

#emph[Hope] is the anchor, which holdeth us fast against all the storms of Satan, so as they can never drive us out of our harbour, which is the Lord Jesus Christ.

#emph[Love] is the glue and solder which maketh us one with Christ: for it is the property of love to unite those that love one another in one. #emph[Jonathans soul was knit with the soul of] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 18. 1.] #emph[David.] For why? #emph[Jonathan loved him as his own soul.] He that loveth is well pleased with him whom he loveth, and seeketh also to please him, that they may mutually delight one in another. Were these three virtues well rooted in us, we would say, #emph[who shall separate us from the love of Christ? Shall tribulation,] #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 8. 35. #emph[etc.]] #emph[or distress,] etc.

=== §. 88. #emph[Of the equal privilege of all the Saints.]

III. Concerning the phrase, whereby the nearness of #footnote[All the Saints made one Spouse.] man and wife is set forth #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] it may be demanded how this can be applied to Christ and the Saints, who are more then two?

#emph[Answ.] Christ by one Spirit knitteth us all into one body, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 12. 13.] and so maketh all jointly considered together one Spouse. The multitude of Saints doth no more imply many wives, then the multitude of members which the natural body of a wife hath. This point then teacheth us, that

#emph[In the mystical marriage betwixt Christ and the Church, all and every of the Saints have an equal privilege.] Some are not #footnote[See §. 22.] Concubines, some wives, nor some more loved, or preferred to another, but all one wife. #emph[All are one in Christ Jesus.] #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 3. 28.]

Neither the father that gave them all, nor the son who #footnote[Reason.] took them all, saw any thing in one more then in another; their mere grace moved them to do what they did. Well may every one apply all the forenamed privileges unto themselves: and not one emulate another.

This affordeth instruction to the more eminent in the #footnote[Use.] Church, that like proud dames they insult not over others, as if they were their hand-maids: and consolation to the meaner sort, that they may uphold themselves, and possess their souls with patience, and not envy, or grieve at the outward prosperity and privileges of others. In the greatest privilege they are equal to the greatest.

This of the parties coupled to Christ. For these words #emph[(they two)] show that all the Saints are but #emph[one:] Christ is the other of the two. The next words #emph[(are one flesh)] show how near those Saints are to Christ.

=== §. 89. #emph[Of the near union betwixt Christ and the Church.]

The main point here to be noted is, that

#emph[Christ and the Church are most nearly linked together.] What #footnote[#emph[Christus nihil aliud deputat corpus suum (i.) Ecclesiam, quam seipsum, quia de Christo & ecclesia verius intelligitur, Erunt duo in carne una. Aug. de pec. mer. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 31.] can be nearer, then that two should come #emph[into one flesh?]

This is somewhat more then to be #emph[of Christs flesh.] That shows we are as it were cut out of Christ: this shows that we are again knit to him. That was a preparation unto this: this is as the consummation and perfection of all. #footnote[See §. 70.] Many metaphors are used to set forth the near union betwixt Christ and his Church, but this surpasseth them all. As here we and Christ are said to be #emph[one flesh,] so in another place, #emph[one spirit.] Well therefore might the name and title #emph[Christ] be given to this Spouse of Christ.

#footnote[§. 84.] It was noted on a like ground to this, that of all other persons #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 17. #emph[&] 12. 12.] the transgression of a wife against an husband is most heinous. What then are the transgressions of the Church against Christ? As we are much more bound unto Christ for the privileges we receive from him as an #emph[Head] and #emph[husband,] and so our Saviour having made with us an everlasting covenant of marriage, then for those we received from him as our Creator, Lord and master: so are the rebellions now committed against him more monstrous. To #emph[Adam] that broke the first covenant whereby like a rebellious child and servant he sinned against his father and master, mercy and pardon was given: but to such as now break the bond of this everlasting covenant, and make a total and final desertion, utterly renouncing this husband, or by their adultery cause him to give them a bill of divorce, #emph[there remaineth no more sacrifice] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 10. 27.] #emph[for sins; but a certain fearful looking for of judgment, and fiery indignation which shall devour the adversaries.] This is to be noted, to make us the more circumspect over our ways, resisting sin in the beginning, and #emph[looking diligently lest any] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 12. 15.] #emph[man fall from the grace of God;] and #emph[giving no place at all unto] #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 4. 27.] #emph[the Devil.] Satan will most endeavour to dissolve the nearest bonds that God maketh. This then being the nearest of all, we ought to be the most careful in preserving it.
